A||||14||fyerstarter||Code 42, What the ****!!?||||1||
Z||000000||fyerstarter||05-16-2001||11:47 AM||||Allright I just got finished putting a new pickup coil in and the car is still running like **** . <P>So far I have:<P>Replaced the Coil, Module, and Pickup Coil. Had the module tested at Autozone and it's good. The coil is a brand new MSD Blaster, the pickup coil is brand new.<BR>When I pulled the distributor to do the pickup coil I made sure to mark it's position and everything went in as it came out. When the car started up it idled good and doesn't seem to be out of time (maybe a little but nothing huge)<P>So I thought it was fixed. Drove around the block and after about 2 mins it starts cutting out on me again. The idle is erratic but it doesn't vary, it jerks. The tach is all over the place jerking around like it's having a seizure. I barely made it home and the car died in the parking lot and will not start.<P>I checked the codes and got a 42. Ignition timing. What could be causing this? This is the exact problem I had before I pulled the distributor so I know it's not something I put back together wrong. I've heard about electro-static interference between the coil wires and the plug wires so I routed them as far away as I could. Could I just have some plug wires crossed? Should I re-route them or is it worse than that? <P>Is it possible that this new coil is bad? I have the original one I can put back in, but the MSD worked great for a couple of days before this problem.<P>It certainly seems like something sensor/iginition related to me rather than a mechanical problem.<P>Any help would be greatly appreciated. I've already missed 2 days of work working on this thing and I need to get it fixed or start thinking about getting a new car, or a new job, or both :\||||reg||1
Z||000001||RossT||05-16-2001||11:52 AM||||I have not been following your problems, but have you checked the TACH FILTER?  Try searching the tech section.  Many people have had simular problems to only find out after many dolars spent that it was the tach filter.  <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A>  sells them.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wolfhound||05-16-2001||11:58 AM||||Unplug the tach filter and jump the connector. If the problem goes away thats it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fyerstarter||05-16-2001||01:06 PM||||Ok I unplugged the tach filter (did not jump the connecter, does that mean i should make it loop back into itself?) and it started up. That doesn't mean much because it's been starting up and dying a lot lately. It idled for a bit and then started running really crappilly and stalled. So I started it again and now it seems to be idling and revving pretty good. I still get a code 42 though. It's been running for about 10 minutes now and it idles good and when i rev it seems pretty responsive but the check engine light is still there. I also swapped out the oginal working coil (not the blaster).<P>So now I know I have:<P>-Working module<BR>-Working Coil<BR>-Tach filter unplugged<BR>-Working Pickup Coil<BR>-Does not seem to be missing at idle of badly out of time<P>STILL GETTING A CODE 42.<P>I was reading in the Fiero online Service Guide to check a bunch of Wires to the ECM. Is this the next logical step. When it says "check" does that mean check the voltage to them? Or check to see if a rat chewed throught them or something like that?<P>Because I dont have any equipment to check the voltage or anything like that. Not even a lowly test light  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>Oh one more question. There is no way that having the tach filter unplugged could cause the code 42 right?||||reg||1
Z||000004||fyerstarter||05-16-2001||01:33 PM||||ARGGGHH!!<P>Ok I went back out and jumpered the tachfilter connector like WolfHound suggested and it started and ran for about 3 secs and then died. So I un-jumpered it and just left it unplugged like it was before and it still didn't start! Made sure the coil wire is plugged in good to the cap and the coil and its a pretty new wire (about 2 months) and looks in good condition.<P>So it looks like its an inttermittent problem. I'm pretty much out of ideas here.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||01:37 PM||||If you pulled the distributor out then you have to adjust the timing. Putting a mark on the side of the distributor is only to make sure you dont put it back in 180° out - you cant possibly line that mark back up to within 1 or 2°.<P>  You need a timing lite, start the car up and let it warm up till the idle drops to at least 1000 rpm, then jumper the AB on the test connector and set your timing - prob should be 10° before  TDC. Look for the sticker on the engine lid.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wolfhound||05-16-2001||02:11 PM||||fyerstarter, did you jump the connector on the coil side? the circuit goes from the coil into the filter and back out through the connector to the harness. Jump across the connector on the coil side and try it. your just bipassing the filter. jumping the tach filter to it's self will do nothing.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fyerstarter||05-16-2001||03:08 PM||||Yeah thats what I did jumpered on the coil side and it wouldn't start. Un-jumpered it and it still wouldn't start. Then I came inside for about half and hour, went outside right now to check and see if I did it the way you said and now it starts right up. Un-plugged, plugged, jumpered, un-jumpered it starts. But I know it's not going to last, first thing that popped on was that damn code 42 again.<P>I'm thinking at this point it is either a bad ECM or an intermittent short or something. I just don't understand how it works and then doesn't, and then works again. <P>I'm at my wits end here.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||04:24 PM||||intermittant problems are the biggest PITA. when the car is running you could take a screwdriver handle and tap on the connectors, and the sensors, and see if one is flakey.<P>  or bend the wires at the connectors a little to see if thats where its intermittant.<P>  A partially failing wire in the wireing harness is also difficult to nail down, cause as the engine runs and torques the wires bend and flex a little.<P>  Here is a thought. I am pretty sure if you disconnect the MAP sensor then the ECM will switch into limp-home-mode which ignores most of the other sensors and inputs. If the car wont start and you do this, it might help narrow down the problem.<P>  Just to make sure, you are unplugging the ECM power to clear the code, so you are not reading the same code 'event' over and over?<P> Also, you do need to set the timing after pulling the distibutor - that is what the code 42 is telling you - the timing is off.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||05-16-2001||04:33 PM||||You probally have already seen the code 42 check list from the online service guide:<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/codes.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/codes.html</A> <P>It would be worth your time to pull the access to the ECM and either use an ohm meter or a light bulb, battery and long wire and check all those wires and make sure one is not flakey or corroded at the connections.<P>When the car wont start you could also pull a plug wire and see if there is spark to ground while someone cranks it, just for a sanity check. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||fyerstarter||05-16-2001||05:38 PM||||I don't think the code 42 is because the timing is off because that was the initial problem that caused me to pull the distributor and install a new pickup coil. Also I lined it up pretty close when re-installed it and idles pretty good when it does. But anyway it was stalling, getting waky idle, and code 42 before I even touched the distributor. <P>From what I read here <A HREF="http://www.syty.org/d&e-code42.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.syty.org/d&e-code42.html</A>  it sounds like for the first 400 rpms its the ignition modules job to start the car and control the timing and then the ECM takes over. But these functions are controlled by the ECM :\ I know the module is good and the connection to the pickup coil is good because its all new. Maybe the outer connections to the module are bad. I guess Im gonna check those and the ECM, guess i better get myself an ohmeter. I can't check for spark because I have no-one to crank it over for me now but I'm pretty damn sure it's not getting any (when it doesnt start).<P>Thanks for the suggestions. I'll keep ya posted. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Wolfhound||05-16-2001||06:27 PM||||fyerstarter, this is a long shot, but the short two wire connector that goes from the coil to the module may be broken inside the wires insulation. I had this problem once and it's difficult to find. A break will give intermittent contact and is heat sensitive. Mine was broken next to one of the connectors. Remove it to check and you need a continuity tester to check it. <BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||fyerstarter||05-16-2001||08:09 PM||||I checked all the wires going to the ECM, nothing looks corroded or broken. I can't really test teh voltage as I have no meter. <P>I'm gonna check out that coil/module wire now Wolfhound. I dont have a continuity tester but i should be able to just connect it like this right<P><BR>+ battery -------&gt; coil/module wire ------&gt; Test Light &lt;-------- - ground    <P>for each of the wires in the plug to test them right?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Wolfhound||05-16-2001||08:31 PM||||Right, While connected wiggle them around a bit. Pull lightly on connectors.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Ken Wittlief||05-17-2001||10:43 AM||||you can get a cheapo-delux volt-ohm meter from radio shack for about $10. Handy thing to have in your tool box or glove compartment - really small.||||reg||1
